## Test Anti-Pattern Audit — Severity-Ranked Findings

This test suite is a textbook example of **"coverage theater"** — every production method is invoked (achieving line coverage) but almost nothing is actually verified.

---

### 🔴 Critical — Tests that guarantee nothing

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Bug it would miss |
|---|------|-------------|-------------------|
| 1 | `AddProduct_Works` (L17-21) | **Fire-and-forget** — no assert at all | `Save()` could silently discard; you'd never know |
| 2 | `UpdatePrice_Works` (L31-35) | **Fire-and-forget** — calls method, never checks result | Price could remain 9.99 or become negative |
| 3 | `RemoveProduct_Works` (L37-42) | **Fire-and-forget** — never verifies deletion | Product could still exist after "removal" |
| 4 | `GetProductCount_Works` (L63-66) | **Fire-and-forget** — result not even captured in an assert | Count could return -1 and test passes |
| 5 | `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L68-74) | **Fire-and-forget** — most dangerous method, zero verification | Discount could multiply prices, corrupt all data |
| 6 | `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L84-88) | **Fire-and-forget** — never checks the product was persisted | Parsing could silently fail or save garbage |

### 🟠 High — Tests with meaningless assertions

| # | Test | Anti-Pattern | Bug it would miss |
|---|------|-------------|-------------------|
| 7 | `GetProduct_Works` (L23-29) | **Assert.IsNotNull only** — verifies *something* came back, not *what* | Could return wrong product, wrong price, wrong name |
| 8 | `SearchProducts_Works` (L45-52) | **Assert.IsNotNull on a List** — a `List<T>` is never null from LINQ | Could return 0 results or wrong results |
| 9 | `ExportToCsv_Works` (L76-82) | **Assert.IsNotNull on string** — empty string passes | CSV format could be completely malformed |
| 10 | `GetAllProducts_Works` (L55-60) | **Assert.IsNotNull on a List** — same as #8 | Empty list or wrong contents both pass |

---

### Concrete Fixes

```csharp
// #1 — AddProduct: verify round-trip
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_PersistsProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);
    var retrieved = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(product, retrieved);
}

// #2 — UpdatePrice: verify the new price
[TestMethod]
